+# arXiv:1604.02303 — integer-matrix semigroup membership
+
+**Trust status: locally verified, but not yet shippable.** G1–G8 pass. The required
+four-vendor hardening loop and all G9 oracle arms remain unscored because OpenRouter
+returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)` on every attempted call. Those
+errors are infrastructure failures, not model failures, and G9 therefore remains
+explicitly false in `selftest_report.json` until a funded key reruns the harness.
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| native domain / regime | algebra / integer lattice |
+| computational core | other: matrix-semigroup word decomposition |
+| certificate | exact symbolic generator word |
+| intuition | change of variables |
+| essentiality / reduction | native / none |
+
+## The problem
+
+The source is Potapov and Semukhin, [*Decidability of the Membership Problem for
+2×2 integer matrices*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.02303). An instance gives labelled,
+nonsingular 2×2 integer generators and a target matrix. The solver returns an ordered
+word of labels whose exact product is the target. The checker validates labels and
+multiplies four integer entries per factor; it never reads the planted answer.
+
+Generation is inverse, not search: sample a generator word and multiply it to obtain
+the target. Internally the generators are simultaneous unimodular conjugates of
+`[[B,d],[0,1]]`, using distinct hidden digits `0 <= d < B`. All digits are generated
+before the witness is sampled, so there is no separate planted-element population.
+
+## Why Track B
+
+Section 2 fixes membership as equality to a finite generator product. Theorem 1 in
+Section 3 proves decidability for all nonsingular 2×2 integer generators; its following
+complexity remark gives an EXPSPACE upper bound. The proof limits non-unimodular factors
+using `log_2 |det(Target)|` and reduces the remaining choices to regular-language
+emptiness. Section 3.1, Proposition 7 and Corollary 8 make the `GL(2,Z)` case decidable
+through unique canonical `X,N,S,R` words. The introduction also records polynomial
+algorithms for the one-generator orbit problem and commutative generators. These are
+easy regimes and rule out a Track-A claim.
+
+For this bounded, determinant-forced subclass, the successful reference method is
+bidirectional meet-in-the-middle over matrix products. Its complexity is
+`O(n^(ceil(k/2)))` exact states. At the named shipping candidate (`n=14`, `k=10`,
+48-bit radix), eight exact runs averaged **969,520 product nodes and 12.879927 s** each.
+The compact route notices that all generator differences occupy one rank-one line,
+normalizes its endpoints to digits `0` and `B-1`, and reads the target coordinate in
+base `B`; the implementation counts **181 exact arithmetic operations**. Thus an
+efficient structured decoder exists and is disclosed—this family tests whether a
+no-tool solver finds and executes that compression, not structural complexity.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+With `make_instance(n=4, word_length=3, digit_bits=5, seed=7)` the full data are:
+
+```text
+g00 = [[-2249,1050],[-4875,2276]]
+g01 = [[-689,330],[-1495,716]]
+g02 = [[-1313,618],[-2847,1340]]
+g03 = [[-299,150],[-650,326]]
+Target = [[-1541189,719430],[-3339245,1558766]]
+```
+
+The answer is `<answer>["g00","g01","g00"]</answer>`.
+`verify(inst, inst["answer"])` returns `(True, "ok")`; deleting the last label returns
+`(False, "wrong word length: determinants require 3 labels, got 2")`. A person can
+solve this demo on paper by checking at most `4^3=64` words, or much faster by finding
+the affine coordinate.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | generators `n` | length `k` | digit bits | word space | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 4 | 3 | 5 | 64 | hand-scale illustration |
+| easy | 10 | 8 | 32 | 100,000,000 | oracle run blocked before scoring |
+| medium | 12 | 10 | 40 | 61,917,364,224 | not reached |
+| hard | 14 | 10 | 48 | 289,254,654,976 | named shipping candidate; locally tested |
+
+`SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY` currently names `hard`. Once the bare harness can run, its held
+rung—not this provisional choice—must determine the final shipping setting.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | measured result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 | 20/20 planted witnesses verify; every answer is JSON-native |
+| G2 | 5/5 corruptions rejected with five distinct diagnostics |
+| G3 | model-style prose and fenced tagged JSON round-trips |
+| G4 | 0/200,000 structure-aware uniform words valid (`< 10^-6`) |
+| G5 | demo has exactly 1 valid word; shipping density 0/200,000; strongest failing attack used 32,768 candidates in 3.440213 s |
+| G6 | four attacks, 0/8 successes each; reference MITM solves 8/8 |
+| G7 | doubling `n` to 28 increases the space while leaving the 10-token answer fixed |
+| G8 | 100/100 symmetry checks and 100/100 carried witnesses pass; 20/20 unrelated keys differ |
+| G9 | **pending/fail**: 61 chars, 16 estimated tokens, 10 atoms, 181 route operations; oracle polarity not measured |
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+| run | preset | scored solved/attempts | result |
+|---|---|---:|---|
+| bare STEP 4 | easy | 0/0 | four redraws all received OpenRouter HTTP 403; harness aborted |
+| G9 hinted | hard candidate | 0/0 | not run because the same account quota is exhausted |
+| G9 placebo | hard candidate | 0/0 | not run because the same account quota is exhausted |
+
+There is no hinted-minus-placebo estimate yet. No conclusion about oracle hardness or
+the usefulness of the structural hint is justified from the current evidence.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from gen_1604_02303 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, render, parse_answer, verify
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=7, **DIFFICULTY["demo"])
+print(render(inst))
+answer = parse_answer('<answer>["g00","g01","g00"]</answer>')
+assert verify(inst, answer) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+After a valid `hardened` transcript exists, emit with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 1604.02303 20 hard
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+This is a deliberately structured subset of the paper’s general membership problem.
+Software that tests affine collinearity solves it quickly; the measured million-state
+MITM gap applies only when that structure is missed. The `0/200,000` guess rate is for
+uniform length-10 words, not for an expert prior informed by matrix entries. The panel
+did not implement the paper’s full canonical-automaton EXPSPACE procedure, an SMT
+encoding, or lattice reduction. Large exact divisions are part of the 181-operation
+compact route, so some no-tool failures may reflect arithmetic reliability as well as
+failure to see the change of variables. The canonical key covers generator relabelling,
+both determinant signs of unimodular conjugation, their composition, and transposition
+with word reversal; it is not a complete invariant for arbitrary semigroup isomorphism.
