Implemented a native Track B generator based on the paper’s matrix-semigroup membership problem and [Theorem 1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1604.02303).

- [Generator module](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1604.02303/gen_1604_02303.py)
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1604.02303/README.md)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1604.02303/selftest_report.json)
- [Bare oracle transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1604.02303/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [Hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1604.02303/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [Placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1604.02303/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

Local results:

- G1–G8 pass.
- Four attacks succeed on 0/8 instances each.
- Reference meet-in-the-middle solves 8/8, averaging 969,520 product nodes and about 11.63 million arithmetic operations.
- Compact affine-coordinate route: 181 operations.
- Guessing: 0/200,000.
- Answer: 61 characters, 10 elements.

The result is not yet shippable: every oracle call returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)`. Consequently G9 remains explicitly false and `all_passed` is false. The error transcripts are preserved; this is not recorded as a paper rejection. A funded `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` is required to rerun the bare and G9 arms.
