The audit found a real hardness bug in the existing construction: because the old “digits” were distinct modulo the common determinant, trying each generator as the leftmost factor and checking whether the quotient stayed integral recovered every word greedily in \(O(nk)\). That makes the million-state meet-in-the-middle baseline misleading.

I’m changing the inverse construction so all generators are congruent deeply enough modulo the determinant that every wrong prefix remains integral until the terminal equality check. This preserves the paper-native matrix membership problem and the short affine-coordinate route, while removing the cheap divisibility leak. I’ll add that attack explicitly to G6 and remeasure all costs.
