     1	# arXiv:1604.02303 — integer-matrix semigroup membership
     2	
     3	**Trust status: locally verified, but not yet shippable.** G1–G8 pass. The required
     4	four-vendor hardening loop and all G9 oracle arms remain unscored because OpenRouter
     5	returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)` on every attempted call. Those
     6	errors are infrastructure failures, not model failures, and G9 therefore remains
     7	explicitly false in `selftest_report.json` until a funded key reruns the harness.
     8	
     9	| profile field | value |
    10	|---|---|
    11	| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
    12	| native domain / regime | algebra / integer lattice |
    13	| computational core | other: matrix-semigroup word decomposition |
    14	| certificate | exact symbolic generator word |
    15	| intuition | change of variables |
    16	| essentiality / reduction | native / none |
    17	
    18	## The problem
    19	
    20	The source is Potapov and Semukhin, [*Decidability of the Membership Problem for
    21	2×2 integer matrices*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.02303). An instance gives labelled,
    22	nonsingular 2×2 integer generators and a target matrix. The solver returns an ordered
    23	word of labels whose exact product is the target. The checker validates labels and
    24	multiplies four integer entries per factor; it never reads the planted answer.
    25	
    26	Generation is inverse, not search: sample a generator word and multiply it to obtain
    27	the target. Internally the generators are simultaneous unimodular conjugates of
    28	`[[B,r+B^k*d],[0,1]]`, using distinct hidden radix digits `0 <= d < B` and an
    29	offset `r` divisible by `B-1`. All digits are generated before the witness is sampled,
    30	so there is no separate planted-element population. The common residue modulo `B^k`
    31	is deliberate: testing whether `G^-1*Target` is integral leaves every possible prefix
    32	alive through depth `k`, rather than revealing the next planted label.
    33	
    34	## Why Track B
    35	
    36	Section 2 fixes membership as equality to a finite generator product. Theorem 1 in
    37	Section 3 proves decidability for all nonsingular 2×2 integer generators; its following
    38	complexity remark gives an EXPSPACE upper bound. The proof limits non-unimodular factors
    39	using `log_2 |det(Target)|` and reduces the remaining choices to regular-language
    40	emptiness. Section 3.1, Proposition 7 and Corollary 8 make the `GL(2,Z)` case decidable
    41	through unique canonical `X,N,S,R` words. The introduction also records polynomial
    42	algorithms for the one-generator orbit problem and commutative generators. These are
    43	easy regimes and rule out a Track-A claim.
    44	
    45	For this bounded, determinant-forced subclass, the successful reference method is
    46	bidirectional meet-in-the-middle over matrix products. Its complexity is
    47	`O(n^(ceil(k/2)))` exact states. At the named shipping candidate (`n=14`, `k=10`,
    48	48-bit radix), eight exact runs averaged **871,963 product nodes, about 10,463,560
    49	scalar arithmetic operations, and 5.341744 s** each.
    50	The compact route notices that all generator differences occupy one rank-one line,
    51	normalizes its endpoints to digits `0` and `B-1`, and reads the target coordinate in
    52	base `B`; the implementation counts **181 exact arithmetic operations**. Thus an
    53	efficient structured decoder exists and is disclosed—this family tests whether a
    54	no-tool solver finds and executes that compression, not structural complexity.
    55	
    56	## Worked demo
    57	
    58	With `make_instance(n=4, word_length=3, digit_bits=5, seed=7)`, `render` returns:
    59	
    60	```text
    61	Find a membership witness in a semigroup of nonsingular 2 x 2 integer matrices.
    62	
    63	Definitions. Multiplication is ordinary left-to-right matrix multiplication over the integers. The semigroup generated by the displayed matrices consists of the identity and every finite product of one or more generators; order matters and a generator may be repeated. A membership witness is the ordered word of generator labels whose product equals the target exactly. Matrix rows and entries are shown as [[a,b],[c,d]].
    64	
    65	There are 4 generators. Their labels are g00 through g03.
    66	  g00 = [[-306948799, 2592012300], [-36349200, 306948826]]
    67	  g01 = [[-66509119, 561632780], [-7876080, 66509146]]
    68	  g02 = [[-162684991, 1373784588], [-19265328, 162685018]]
    69	  g03 = [[-6399199, 54037900], [-757800, 6399226]]
    70	
    71	Target = [[-4799187199, 40526618100], [-568324800, 4799204776]]
    72	
    73	For this instance, the absolute determinant of every generator is the same integer B>1, while |det(Target)|=B^3. Consequently every valid word has exactly 3 labels. Return exactly 3 labels in multiplication order. Repetition is allowed; generator indices are 0-based; list order is significant.
    74	
    75	Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as a JSON list of quoted generator labels.
    76	Example format: <answer>["g00","g01","g02"]</answer>
    77	Output nothing else inside the tags.
    78	```
    79	
    80	The answer is `<answer>["g00","g03","g03"]</answer>`.
    81	`verify(inst, inst["answer"])` returns `(True, "ok")`; deleting the last label returns
    82	`(False, "wrong word length: determinants require 3 labels, got 2")`. A person can
    83	solve this demo on paper by checking at most `4^3=64` words, or much faster by finding
    84	the affine coordinate.
    85	
    86	## Difficulty presets
    87	
    88	| preset | generators `n` | length `k` | digit bits | word space | status |
    89	|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
    90	| demo | 4 | 3 | 5 | 64 | hand-scale illustration |
    91	| easy | 10 | 8 | 32 | 100,000,000 | oracle run blocked before scoring |
    92	| medium | 12 | 10 | 40 | 61,917,364,224 | not reached |
    93	| hard | 14 | 10 | 48 | 289,254,654,976 | named shipping candidate; locally tested |
    94	
    95	`SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY` currently names `hard`. Once the bare harness can run, its held
    96	rung—not this provisional choice—must determine the final shipping setting.
    97	
    98	## Gate results
    99	
   100	| gate | measured result |
   101	|---|---|
   102	| G1 | 20/20 planted witnesses verify; every answer is JSON-native |
   103	| G2 | 5/5 corruptions rejected with five distinct diagnostics |
   104	| G3 | model-style prose and fenced tagged JSON round-trips |
   105	| G4 | 0/200,000 structure-aware uniform words valid (`< 10^-6`) |
   106	| G5 | demo has exactly 1 valid word; shipping density 0/200,000; reference MITM averaged 871,963 nodes / 10,463,560 operations / 5.341744 s; strongest failing attack used 32,768 candidates |
   107	| G6 | five attacks, 0/8 successes each; reference MITM solves 8/8; all 85 demo prefix states retain integral quotients and only one terminal reaches identity |
   108	| G7 | doubling `n` to 28 increases the space while leaving the 10-token answer fixed |
   109	| G8 | 100/100 symmetry checks and 100/100 carried witnesses pass; 20/20 unrelated keys differ |
   110	| G9 | **pending/fail**: 61 chars, 16 estimated tokens, 10 atoms, 181 route operations; oracle polarity not measured |
   111	
   112	## Oracle loop and G9 arms
   113	
   114	| run | preset | scored solved/attempts | result |
   115	|---|---|---:|---|
   116	| bare STEP 4 | easy | 0/0 | four redraws all received OpenRouter HTTP 403; harness aborted |
   117	| G9 hinted | hard candidate | 0/0 | four calls attempted; every call received the same account-wide HTTP 403 |
   118	| G9 placebo | hard candidate | 0/0 | four calls attempted; every call received the same account-wide HTTP 403 |
   119	
   120	There is no hinted-minus-placebo estimate yet. No conclusion about oracle hardness or
   121	the usefulness of the structural hint is justified from the current evidence.
   122	
   123	## Use
   124	
   125	```python
   126	from gen_1604_02303 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, render, parse_answer, verify
   127	
   128	inst = make_instance(seed=7, **DIFFICULTY["demo"])
   129	print(render(inst))
   130	answer = parse_answer('<answer>["g00","g03","g03"]</answer>')
   131	assert verify(inst, answer) == (True, "ok")
   132	```
   133	
   134	After a valid `hardened` transcript exists, emit with:
   135	
   136	```bash
   137	bash scripts/emit.sh 1604.02303 20 hard
   138	```
   139	
   140	## Caveats
   141	
   142	This is a deliberately structured subset of the paper’s general membership problem.
   143	Software that tests affine collinearity solves it quickly; the measured million-state
   144	MITM gap applies only when that structure is missed. The `0/200,000` guess rate is for
   145	uniform length-10 words, not for an expert prior informed by matrix entries. The panel
   146	does include the formerly fatal integral-left-quotient attack, but did not implement
   147	the paper’s full canonical-automaton EXPSPACE procedure, an SMT encoding, or lattice
   148	reduction. Large exact divisions are part of the 181-operation compact route, so some
   149	no-tool failures may reflect arithmetic reliability as well as failure to see the
   150	change of variables. The canonical key covers generator relabelling, both determinant
   151	signs of unimodular conjugation, their composition, and transposition with word
   152	reversal; it is not a complete invariant for arbitrary semigroup isomorphism.

